# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from vals import *

H1("5. Conclusion")
P("A physically consistent, SI-unit model of pulsed-laser heating, bioheat transfer, thermoelastic stress and Arrhenius damage in a layered tumour and normal-tissue slab was formulated, verified and used to optimize the laser pulse train. The main conclusions, which are conclusions about the model and carry no clinical claim, are as follows.")
P("First, the numerical method is verified. The finite element solution with second-order backward differentiation agrees with an independent Laplace-domain solution to better than 0.3 per cent in temperature and 0.5 per cent in stress from 3 s on, with larger early-time errors of up to 1.8 per cent in temperature while the first pulse switches on. The mesh convergence of the collateral integral is second order, the Arrhenius quadrature is exact at constant temperature and fourth order on a ramp, and the energy balance closes to $5\\times10^{-5}$ for the Pennes and MGL models.")
P("Second, inertia is negligible for loading times of the order of a second. With inertia retained, the temperature changes by less than $10^{-11}$ in relative terms and the axial stress is below $2\\times10^{-9}$ of the in-plane stress. In a traction-free one-dimensional slab the quasi-static axial stress vanishes identically, so the safety stress that matters is the in-plane stress under lateral constraint, which is proportional to the temperature and, in the modified Green–Lindsay theory, to the temperature plus the strain-rate lag times the temperature rate.")
P("Third, the optimum is determined mainly by the thermal tail. More than 99 per cent of the damage at the tumour edge is accumulated after the laser has been switched off, the collateral objective is almost independent of the number of pulses (a spread below 1 per cent), and the freedom in the pulse train can be used for secondary goals. The result depends on the evaluation horizon, which changes the objective by 13 to 14 per cent when it is halved or raised to 300 s, and the one-dimensional geometry exaggerates the tail.")
P("Fourth, the choice among the Pennes, Cattaneo–Vernotte and modified Green–Lindsay models changes the safe dose by less than 1 per cent at a relaxation time of 2 s and by about 4 per cent at 20 s, but the Moore–Gibson–Thompson type thermal-displacement term changes the dose by 4 to 12 per cent, depending on its coefficient, and the collateral damage by a factor of 2.4. The source values of the parameter $k^*$ are unstable with relaxation times of seconds, since the stability condition $\\tau_qk^*<k$ requires $\\tau_q<0.046$ s, and a rescaled coefficient was used.")
P("Fifth, the sensitivity analysis shows that the Arrhenius parameters of both tissues dominate the collateral damage, by a factor of 6.7 for a tenfold change of the frequency factor of the normal tissue, whereas perfusion, the relaxation times and the optical properties each change it by less than 10 per cent. The stress limit becomes infeasible below 29.3 kPa for the horizon used, and a long strain-rate lag raises the peak stress above the limit of 36 kPa.")
P("The main recommendation for future work is to replace the illustrative Arrhenius, perfusion and relaxation parameters by measured values, to validate the model against temperature measurements in a controlled phantom or tissue, to extend the model to three dimensions so that the lateral heat loss and the lateral stress are represented, and to add a second damage mechanism for the vasculature. Until then the numbers in this paper should be read as properties of the model.")
